{
  "requirement": "When authoring evidence for a named example, its complete fenced declaration\nMUST be included in the mandatory input set defined by\n{cite}`requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs`.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Judgment:** `requirements.examples.authoring_inputs` is satisfied. One test gap is noted below. I judged it minor and did not treat it as a failure.\n\n**Implementation**\n- `AssessmentSources::prepare` in `src/authoring/inputs.rs` (lines 43–182) is marked `{impl}` for this requirement. It accepts any non-term definition, so a named example is a valid subject.\n- It always makes `InputSelector::Requirements { requirements: subject_name }` the first selector. After source resolution, that selector becomes the first entry of the mandatory input set.\n- The `Requirements` selector resolves in `InputSelector::resolve` (`src/inputs.rs`). Each matched definition contributes `definition.raw_lines`, which `collect_definitions` in `src/evidence.rs` builds from `block.start..block.end`. That range runs from the opening `:::{example} name` fence to the closing `:::`, so the text is the complete fenced declaration.\n- Only the leading `:impl:` and `:test:` option lines are stripped from that text. Those anchors are not lost, because `prepare` turns them into their own source inputs.\n- The set is deduplicated and unresolvable inputs error before inference. Generated `evidence/` content is omitted by default, as `requirements.authoring.mandatory_inputs` specifies.\n- `prepare` is also marked `{impl}` for every other contribution it makes, such as references, terms and anchors. No behavior beyond the requirement appears unmarked.\n\n**Tests**\n- The `{test}` reference is `bundle_combines_direct_references_terms_and_current_existing_selectors` (`src/authoring/inputs.rs`, line 484).\n- It declares `:::{example} case` in the fixture, calls `prepare(.., \"requirements.case\")`, and asserts the following:\n  - `subject_kind` is `Definition(Example)`.\n  - `mandatory_inputs()[0][\"requirements\"] == \"requirements.case\"`.\n- The same test also checks that the example appears as an input when the subject is the requirement `ready` that it tests.\n- The exact fenced text is asserted for requirement subjects in `unresolvable_earlier_inputs_do_not_block_reassessment` and `anchored_files_contribute_root_node_inputs`. The expected text there is `[\":::{req} ready\", \"...\", \":::\"]`.\n- `requirements_inputs_track_matched_declarations_in_full_name_order` in `src/evidence.rs` asserts that stored example input text is the complete fenced declaration.\n- Examples and requirements share the same selector and resolution path, so the assertion on the example's first input plus these tests together cover the scenario.\n\n**Gap:** The example test does not directly assert the example's fence lines in the text. I consider this minor because the code path is shared and the fenced text is asserted elsewhere. No FIXME or acknowledged gaps exist.",
